T*
(4\) WHY EXO ENABLES PARALLEL LLVM AND LIBRARY/BENCHMARK DEVELOPMENT) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(EXO is a **staging layer** that removes the immediate dependency on a full LLVM Mojo-V) Tj
T*
(language+codegen port.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(PARALLELIZATION EFFECT) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- **Compiler/backend team** can work on true first-class LLVM support \(IR typing, ABI, codegen,) Tj
T*
(optimization legality, debugging/profiling integration\).) Tj
T*
(- **Library + benchmark teams** can simultaneously develop encrypted algorithms, APIs, and test) Tj
T*
(suites now using EXO wrappers and intrinsics.) Tj
T*
(- **System team** can validate Mojo-V ISA behavior in Spike and across encryption modes while) Tj
T*
(compiler work is still in progress.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Because EXO is header-first and macro-configurable, most application-facing experimentation can) Tj
T*
(proceed independently from heavyweight compiler milestones.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(5\) TRADE-OFFS: EXO LIBRARY VS. FULL LLVM PORT) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(A. BENEFITS OF EXO \(SHORT/MEDIUM TERM\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(1. **Fast time-to-productivity**) Tj
T*
(   - Usable immediately with existing C/C++ toolchains and simulator workflows.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(2. **Low integration risk early on**) Tj
T*
(   - Avoids front-loading complex compiler architecture changes before ISA and runtime behavior) Tj
T*
(stabilize.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(3. **Great for bring-up and semantic validation**) Tj
T*
(   - Easy to build focused tests that verify operators, type conversions, and encrypted control) Tj
T*
(primitives.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(4. **Flexible encryption-format experimentation**) Tj
T*
(   - Storage-type macros let teams compare fast/strong/proof-carrying choices from mostly) Tj
T*
(shared code.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(B. COSTS/LIMITS OF EXO \(COMPARED TO FULL LLVM SUPPORT\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(1. **Not a first-class language type system integration**) Tj
T*
(   - Encrypted semantics live in library wrappers, not native compiler IR types and analyses.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(2. **Optimization ceiling**) Tj
ET
