(   - Compiler cannot reason globally about encrypted semantics as deeply as with dedicated LLVM) Tj
T*
(IR and passes.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(3. **Tooling ergonomics gap**) Tj
T*
(   - Diagnostics, debugging, and profiling are less seamless than true compiler-native) Tj
T*
(features.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(4. **Potential abstraction/ABI friction**) Tj
T*
(   - Wrapper indirection and explicit intrinsics can create edge-case interoperability concerns) Tj
T*
(with generic libraries and mixed-language boundaries.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(5. **Long-term maintenance duplication risk**) Tj
T*
(   - Some EXO patterns may later be superseded by compiler-native implementations, requiring) Tj
T*
(migration.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(C. BENEFITS OF FULL LLVM PORT \(LONG TERM\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(A complete LLVM port would enable:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Native encrypted types and operations in the compiler pipeline) Tj
T*
(- Better legality-aware optimization and scheduling) Tj
T*
(- Cleaner source-level language integration beyond wrapper idioms) Tj
T*
(- More robust ecosystem/toolchain support at scale) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(D. PRACTICAL CONCLUSION) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(A realistic engineering strategy is:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(1. Use EXO now for rapid bring-up, validation, and benchmark acceleration.) Tj
T*
(2. Continue full LLVM integration in parallel.) Tj
T*
(3. Gradually transition performance-critical and production-facing paths to compiler-native) Tj
T*
(support as it matures.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(In short: **EXO optimizes for velocity and parallel progress; a full LLVM port optimizes for) Tj
T*
(long-term compiler quality, portability, and scale.**) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(6\) BOTTOM LINE) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(The Mojo-V EXO library is a deliberate bridge between ISA innovation and production compiler) Tj
T*
(maturity. It gives teams a usable encrypted programming model today, proves semantics with real) Tj
T*
(benchmarks, and de-risks the path to eventual first-class LLVM support.) Tj
